/P 950 0 R
/ActualText (Renewable Energy \(RE\) Contract Intermediary Agency)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
325 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 950 0 R
/ActualText (This would be managed by the Multilateral Investment Guarantee Agency \(MIGA\) and act as an intermediary for renewable energy contracts. It would increase investment certainty by guaranteeing revenue for renewable energy projects, much like the Solar Energy Corporation of India \(SECI\) does for solar projects in India. This global facility would secure payments for renewable energy projects through mechanisms such as capacity tariff auctions and Contracts for Difference, providing a reliable financial structure for climate policy commitments.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
326 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 210 0 R
/Pg 15 0 R
>>
endobj
327 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 951 0 R
/ActualText (Two-Stage Project Finance Model)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
328 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 951 0 R
/ActualText (Managed by either MIGA or the International Finance Corporation \(IFC\), this model would support low-capacity countries. It would begin with equity and loan financing, followed by the issuance of bonds secured by guaranteed future cash flows from renewable energy projects. Pension funds could also invest in these securitized cash flows, providing long-term, stable financing for green projects.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
329 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 210 0 R
/Pg 15 0 R
>>
endobj
330 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 952 0 R
/ActualText (Energy Contracts for Debt-Distressed Countries)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
331 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 952 0 R
/ActualText (This function would provide financial services to countries in debt distress, such as SIDS, using a 'pay-as-you-go' energy service contract. This model ensures that these countries can develop net-zero energy systems without increasing their debt burden. By transitioning to cleaner and more stable energy sources, these countries could reduce their reliance on expensive imported fossil fuels, lowering both costs and fiscal risks. The preferred creditor arrangement of the World Bank Group \(WBG\) would protect investors from losses, ensuring that delays in payments are managed without affecting the financial stability of the projects.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
