363 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 963 0 R
/ActualText (Emerging markets and developing economies facing difficulties in accessing affordable debt financing for their renewable energy transitions, as well as for research and innovation aimed at reducing GHG emissions. These countries receive risk protection to boost investment flows in the near term and can use national-scale accounts as collateral for borrowing related to climate change mitigation and adaptation. Post-2050, these countries will be the primary beneficiaries of the fund. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
364 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 964 0 R
/ActualText (World Bank Group)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
365 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 965 0 R
/ActualText (The WBG manages the trust in accordance with its Trust Fund Policy \(2021\), ensuring both financial returns and meaningful climate impact.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
366 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 966 0 R
/ActualText (Donors )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
367 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 967 0 R
/ActualText (Wealthy donor countries provide the initial funding for the trust. Donors also hold voting shares, giving them decision-making power within the trust.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
368 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 968 0 R
/ActualText (Investors)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
369 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 969 0 R
/ActualText (Private investors are allowed to contribute to the trust but are required to pay a percentage of their investment returns as a fee. By de-risking investments in the early stages of projects in developing countries, the trust encourages greater private sector investment.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
